  - [1m[38;5;153mweb/[0m — the frontend, replacing the current empty [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  [1mThe load-bearing invariant[0m, and the thing I'd most want a reviewer to check: [3ma task mutation and its event append [0m
  [3mhappen in the same SQLite transaction.[0m If those can ever come apart, the event log silently stops being a truthful
  record of what happened — and since notifications will be built entirely from that log, a missing event becomes a
  notification that never fires, with nothing to point at afterward. Enforcing this is why [38;5;153mrepo/[39m is the sole writer; if
  mutations could be issued from anywhere, the invariant would be a convention instead of a structural guarantee.

  [1mSection 2 — Data model[0m

  users              id, email, display_name, created_at
  sessions           token, user_id, expires_at
  magic_link_tokens  token, email, expires_at, consumed_at

  tasks              id, title, status, creator_id,
                     assignee_id (nullable), created_at, updated_at

  task_events        id, task_id, actor_id, type,
                     from_value, to_value, created_at

  Three event types to start: [38;5;153mtask.created[39m, [38;5;153mtask.status_changed[39m, [38;5;153mtask.assignee_changed[39m. That's the complete set of
  things that can happen to a task under the model you picked, which is a nice property — it means notifications later
  has a closed, enumerable list of triggers rather than an open-ended one.

  Two decisions I made rather than ask, both easy to reverse:

  - [1mStatus is [38;5;153mopen | done[39m.[0m No [38;5;153min_progress[39m. I can add it in one migration plus a UI change if you want it; I left it out
    because you trimmed the task model aggressively elsewhere and I read that as deliberate.
  - [1m[38;5;153mmagic_link_tokens[39m is separate from [38;5;153msessions[0m — one is single-use and short-lived, the other is long-lived, and
    merging them tends to produce confused expiry logic.

  Does this look right so far? Once you're happy with these two, I'll cover the API surface and data flow, error
  handling, and the testing approach — then write it up as a spec for you to review before any code gets written.

